`! tells the parser that this is a builtin command`
!log "Hello, World!";
!log 100;

`Make a string variable called "newvar" with an allowance of 16 bits`
make newVar str[256];
set newVar "This is a string!";


`
On second thought... maybe I bring back the implicit type declarations.
Many parameters will require strings and I don't want to have quotes everywhere.
If i void quotes, how will the parser know the difference between integers and strings?
`

`
Instead of using implicit types like int8, int16 and so on, the parser should only need
to allocate as much room as it needs in implicit storage as it needs for that value.

For declaring types during runtime, specifying how much space is more appropriate.
`

`Possible builtin dynamic memory commands`
!alloc int:8;
`Change the fourth byte to 64`;
!write int:4 int:64;
`Reference the eighth byte in memory`
!log mem:8;